  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Kursbuch",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Kursbuch\"**\n\nThe planner reads like a Swiss printed timetable. Loops and settings are rows in strict columns, figures are tabular, and structure is drawn with ink and hairline rules rather than with boxes floating over the map. One paper column, 400px wide, sits flush against the left edge at full height; the map fills everything else. On a phone the same column becomes the bottom sheet with a square top edge.\n\nDensity is the point. Rows are 28px, cell padding is 6px, section padding is 10–12px. The system is monochrome (one warm-grey neutral scale from paper to ink) and spends colour only where it carries meaning: signal red for the active loop and the one primary action, dark red and green for the words \"avoid\" and \"prefer\", amber for warnings, and the map's own palette underneath. It replaces the D43 rounded-card consumer look (D114); rounded floating cards with soft shadows and roomy padding are the confirmed anti-reference.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One flush paper column, ruled into sections by 1px ink lines.",
      "Visible column rules: 1px hairlines between every column of a table row.",
      "Square corners everywhere; no shadows on UI surfaces.",
      "Ink is the pressable colour; the selected state is inverted (ink ground, paper text).",
      "Signal red is rare: the active loop on the map, its score, the primary button, the caret.",
      "State is shown by line form (a dashed rule for stale results), not by hue."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Signal Rule",
        "body": "Red marks exactly one thing per screen: the active loop (its map line and score) and the action that produces loops. Never use signal red for a heading, a link, a decoration or a second button.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ink-Is-Pressable Rule",
        "body": "Buttons, segments, toggles and fields are drawn in ink. Selected means inverted: ink ground, paper text.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Rule",
        "body": "Every figure is tabular (`font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` on body) and right-aligned in its column.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Narrow Head Rule",
        "body": "Archivo Narrow is for column heads and cell labels only. Never set body copy, buttons or values in it.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ruled Not Floated Rule",
        "body": "Separate things with a 1px rule, not with a shadow, gap or card. A section ends with an ink line.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do put new controls in the 400px column as ruled rows on the 28px rhythm with visible column rules.",
      "Do draw pressable things in ink and show selection by inverting to ink with paper text.",
      "Do separate sections with a 1px ink rule and rows with a 1px hairline.",
      "Do set column heads and cell labels in Archivo Narrow 700 12px, muted, sentence case.",
      "Do show changed state by line form (dashed, solid) before reaching for a new colour.",
      "Do keep the map palette and the cost ramp exactly as tokenised; the route must read above the map."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use rounded corners, floating cards or drop shadows on UI surfaces.",
      "Don't spend signal red on anything but the active loop and the primary action.",
      "Don't fill rows or cells with avoid or prefer colours; they colour the effect word only.",
      "Don't pad roomily: 12px section inset, 6px cell padding, 28px rows."
    ]
  }
